 "moxie-11": {
  "n": 11,
  "file": "Session_11_Parking_Assistant.docx",
  "unitLabel": "SESSION 11 · UNIT 3: DISPLAYS, TIME, SENSORS & MOTION",
  "title": "Parking Assistant",
  "based": "Based on TeachEngineering: Designing a Distance-Sensing Product",
  "mission": [
   "Use an ultrasonic sensor to build a car parking assistant: green when far away, yellow when getting close, and red + beeping when you’re about to hit something."
  ],
  "need": [
   "ESP32 board + USB-C cable",
   "Breadboard",
   "HC-SR04 ultrasonic sensor",
   "1 kΩ (brown, black, red) + 2 kΩ (red, black, red)",
   "Green, yellow, red LEDs + 3 × 220 Ω",
   "Passive buzzer",
   "Ruler or meter stick"
  ],
  "before": [
   "Unplug the USB cable before wiring.",
   "The sensor needs 5 V from VIN.",
   "The Echo pin MUST go through the 1 kΩ / 2 kΩ divider. Echo sends out 5 V, and the divider drops it to about 3.3 V so the ESP32 isn’t damaged.",
   "Ask your instructor to check the divider before you plug in."
  ],
  "test": [
   "Open the Serial Monitor. Move your hand toward the sensor. Does the distance shrink?",
   "Check each zone: far = green, close = yellow, too close = red + beep.",
   "Fill in the calibration table below using a ruler."
  ],
  "stuck": [
   "Always 999? Check Trig (D5), Echo (divider → D4), and VCC on VIN.",
   "Jumpy numbers? Point the sensor at a flat, hard surface like a book.",
   "No beep? Check the buzzer is passive and on D23."
  ],
  "concept": {
   "heading": "HOW THE ULTRASONIC SENSOR WORKS",
   "text": [
    "Like a bat: the sensor sends out a sound too high for us to hear and times how long the echo takes to bounce back. Sound travels about 0.0343 cm per microsecond. The sound goes there and back, so we divide by 2:",
    "distance (cm) = echo time × 0.0343 ÷ 2"
   ],
   "tables": []
  },
  "build": {
   "notes": [
    "Copy it hole for hole. Each wire’s color matches its part. Red wires = 3V3 power, black wires = GND. The red wire from VIN is 5V: it goes ONLY to the sensor’s VCC pin.",
    "Pin names match your board: the pin labeled D25 on your ESP32 is 25 in your code."
   ],
   "tables": [
    [
     [
      "Sensor pin",
      "Connects to",
      "Part",
      "Connects"
     ],
     [
      "VCC",
      "VIN (5V)",
      "Green LED",
      "D25 (+ 220 Ω)"
     ],
     [
      "Trig",
      "D5",
      "Yellow LED",
      "D26 (+ 220 Ω)"
     ],
     [
      "Echo",
      "1 kΩ → D4, and D4 → 2 kΩ → GND",
      "Red LED",
      "D27 (+ 220 Ω)"
     ],
     [
      "Gnd",
      "GND",
      "Buzzer",
      "D23 (+), GND (−)"
     ]
    ]
   ]
  },
  "code": {
   "intro": "readDistanceCm() is a helper function that does the measuring. Fill in the blanks to pick the zones.",
   "lines": [
    "// Names: ______________ & ______________",
    "// Session 11 - Parking Assistant",
    "// What this program does: ______________________________",
    "const int TRIG = 5;",
    "const int ECHO = 4;               // through the voltage divider!",
    "const int GREEN = 25;  const int YELLOW = 26;  const int RED = 27;",
    "const int BUZZER = 23;",
    "void setup() {",
    "  pinMode(TRIG, OUTPUT);  pinMode(ECHO, INPUT);",
    "  pinMode(GREEN, OUTPUT); pinMode(YELLOW, OUTPUT); pinMode(RED, OUTPUT);",
    "  Serial.begin(115200);",
    "}",
    "float readDistanceCm() {          // helper: measures distance",
    "  digitalWrite(TRIG, LOW);  delayMicroseconds(2);",
    "  digitalWrite(TRIG, HIGH); delayMicroseconds(10);   // send a ping",
    "  digitalWrite(TRIG, LOW);",
    "  long duration = pulseIn(ECHO, HIGH, 30000);        // time the echo",
    "  if (duration == 0) return 999;                     // no echo = far away",
    "  return duration * 0.0343 / 2;                      // speed of sound",
    "}",
    "void loop() {",
    "  float cm = readDistanceCm();",
    "  Serial.println(cm);",
    "  digitalWrite(GREEN, LOW); digitalWrite(YELLOW, LOW); digitalWrite(RED, LOW);",
    "  noTone(BUZZER);",
    "  if (cm > ____) {                // far away (more than 30 cm)",
    "    digitalWrite(______, HIGH);",
    "  } else if (cm > ____) {         // getting close (10 to 30 cm)",
    "    digitalWrite(______, HIGH);",
    "  } else {                        // too close! (under 10 cm)",
    "    digitalWrite(______, HIGH);",
    "    tone(BUZZER, 1500);",
    "  }",
    "  delay(100);",
    "}"
   ],
   "remember": "Remember: float holds numbers with decimals · return sends an answer back from a function"
  },
  "stretch": {
   "text": "Make the beeps speed up as you get closer, and show the distance on the OLED. Or try another sensor from your kit (see below).",
   "hint": "Warm alarm: thermistor from 3V3 to D35, matching resistor to GND (code 103 = 10 kΩ, code 104 = 100 kΩ). Remember the first reading, and alarm when it goes up by 150. Night light: photoresistor instead, and the LED turns on when it gets dark."
  },
  "think": "Calibration: ruler 10 cm → sensor ______ ruler 20 cm → sensor ______ ruler 30 cm → sensor ______. Why might they differ?",
  "checkoff": [
   "Echo goes through the 1 kΩ / 2 kΩ divider.",
   "Distance shows in the Serial Monitor.",
   "Green / yellow / red + beep zones all work.",
   "Names, a narrative sentence, and comments are in your code."
  ],
  "explain": "How does the sensor measure distance, and why divide by 2?",
  "signoffs": [
   "Core challenge",
   "Stretch goal"
  ],
  "images": {
   "wiring": "assets/img/moxie/session-11-wiring.png"
  }
 },
